-- R35 - HIS REPLIES ARE SEARCHED ON THEIR OWN: one dedicated Gmail search for his replies to this pipeline's
-- digests, never folded into the inputs search, every hit opened as text, and a footer count that proves it ran.
-- Source: thread "Fix Takeaways reading email replies", 23 Nov 2026. The Opus run of Mon 23 Nov folded the feedback
-- search into its one-day inputs search, opened only the 22 Nov Opus thread (METADATA_ONLY, which carries no text) and
-- printed "no replies on Opus threads in 4 days", while the 21 Nov thread held his "veto P20" (read from the run's
-- session log). The Fable prompt is at its byte cap, so the change goes in as a rule; the Opus loader prompt carries the
-- same step as 3b. Proposed and put in force on 23 Nov 2026 by a single-statement UPDATE. "revert R35" removes it.
select dash.propose_rule($j${"scope": "both", "title": "His email replies are found by their own Gmail search, never folded into the inputs search, and the footer counts what it found", "body": "HIS REPLIES ARE SEARCHED ON THEIR OWN (on Mon 23 Nov 2026 a run folded the feedback search into its one-day inputs search and missed his 21 Nov \"veto P20\").\n\n1. SEARCH. This replaces only the search in the feedback step (Fable STEP 1d, Opus spec 1.9); which messages count, the reply grammar and the FEEDBACK LOG are unchanged. Make ONE Gmail search_threads call of its own, never merged into the inputs search, with query `subject:\"Re: MACRO TAKEAWAYS [Fable\" from:mayank2011@gmail.com newer_than:7d` on the Fable pipeline and `subject:\"Re: MACRO TAKEAWAYS [Opus\" from:mayank2011@gmail.com newer_than:7d` on the Opus pipeline. It returns only threads he has replied to (checked 23 Nov: the Opus query returns the 21 Nov thread and none of the unanswered ones).\n\n2. OPEN. get_thread EVERY thread returned, messageFormat PLAIN_TEXT (never METADATA_ONLY or MINIMAL: they carry no message text), and parse the saved result by script. Apply the feedback step to every message after the first; message ids already in SECTION FEEDBACK LOG are skipped.\n\n3. ALREADY DONE. A Pnn command on a card already closed, void, missed or vetoed, or a Qnn/Tnn command on an item already retired or dropped, changes nothing: log its message id and print \"<id> already <status>\".\n\n4. FAILURE. A search or get_thread error is a failed read, never \"no replies\": retry once; if it still fails print \"feedback: reply search failed\" and carry on.\n\n5. FOOTER. The brief-state line ends its feedback field with \"(reply search: <n> threads, <m> new replies)\", so a run that skipped the search shows it.", "rationale": "No reply to either Takeaways had ever been processed. The 22 Nov fix (open every thread) did not help: the Opus run of 23 Nov never made the four-day feedback search at all; it took the threads from its one-day inputs search, opened the 22 Nov Opus thread metadata-only and concluded there were no replies. A dedicated search on the reply subject cannot be satisfied by the inputs search, returns only threads he answered (cheap: usually zero to two get_thread calls), and the footer count makes a skipped step visible the same morning.", "evidence": "Session log of the Opus run of 23 Nov 2026 (search_threads at 07:04 SGT with newer_than:1d; get_thread 1a2007420fbaba0a METADATA_ONLY at 07:06; footer 'feedback processed: none (no replies on Opus threads in 4 days)'); Gmail thread 1a1fb55fa490e106 holds message 1a1fc69c139e8fae 'veto P20' (21 Nov 12:42 SGT). In-thread test 23 Nov: the Opus query returns only that thread and the parse yields 'veto P20'; the Fable query returns nothing (he has not replied to a Fable digest in 30 days).", "revert_if": "Revert if a run misses a reply that this search should have found, or if the search returns threads he did not reply to.", "review_due": "2026-12-13", "source": "thread"}$j$::jsonb);
